  "leads": {
    "election": [
      "Polling stations closed on turnout of {turnout}%, and by morning the arithmetic was beyond argument: {winner_party} will lead the next government, with {winner_leader} at its head. The talks begin immediately; the promises expire sooner."
    ],
    "confidence_vote": [
      "The government faced its accusers on the floor and the count came back {vfor}-{vagainst}. Survival is not the same as strength — but in this chamber, this month, it will do."
    ],
    "civil_war_out": [
      "It has a name now, the thing everyone feared to say aloud. Armed factions hold ground the state cannot reach, and the government's writ ends where the barricades begin. The republic is at war with itself."
    ],
    "scandal": [
      "{minister}'s resignation letter reached the press before it reached the government. The {portfolio} ministry stands leaderless, {faction} are demanding answers, and the corridors smell of blood."
    ],
    "protest": [
      "They came with banners and left with promises to return. {leader} told the crowd that {faction} would not be ignored — and on the evidence of the numbers in the square, they may be right."
    ],
    "vote_failed": [
      "The government brought \"{bill}\" to the floor believing it had the numbers. It did not. The opposition benches are still cheering."
    ],
    "situation": [
      "The story of the month lands on the government's desk, and every choice has a price. Ministers meet tonight; the country will judge them by morning."
    ],
    "situation_lapsed": [
      "Weeks of silence from the government have let events decide for themselves — and events are rarely generous."
    ],
    "world_axis": [
      "Diplomats have stopped pretending otherwise: {a} and {b} now move as one, and every chancellery on the continent is redrawing its maps."
    ],
    "world_feud": [
      "What began as friction between {a} and {b} has hardened into open enmity. Neighbours are quietly choosing sides."
    ],
    "quiet": [
      "No crisis, no collapse, no scandal worth the ink — a month of government by administration. The nation, suspicious of its own good fortune, reads the small print.",
      "The quietest month in recent memory. Ministers governed, markets traded, and nobody resigned. It will not last."
    ]
  },
